# Resolution
Fixed in commit `75cf693` (`Page cleanup metadata RPC lists`).

What was fixed:
- `ObjectStreamUploadsList` is now a bucket-scoped, paginated RPC instead of a PG-wide all-record response.
- `ObjectCompletedMultipartUploadsList` is now paginated by upload-id marker and served only by the object-PG primary.
- The RPC request codecs enforce a cleanup-specific page limit before server-side scans, and response decoders reject oversized item counts before allocating result vectors.
- Unix clients validate response bucket identity, strict ordering, marker progression, and requested page size before returning cleanup pages.
- Bucket visible-data checks now request a one-row bucket-scoped stream-upload page instead of materializing all stream uploads for a PG.
- Completed multipart tombstone cleanup and pruning now page through each object-PG primary, avoiding the earlier replica/non-primary failure and avoiding unbounded response materialization.
- Regression coverage was added for store pagination, bucket scoping, oversized cleanup-list requests, and malicious oversized response counts.
- `scripts/check-storage-cluster-boundaries` now recognizes the paged mutation-client APIs and continues to reject broad-client regressions.

Already-existing residual:
- `list_stream_upload_sessions_best_effort` still has an explicit local broad `storage_client().list_all_stream_uploads` exception for stale stream-session scavenging. This remains a Phase 10.6 background-worker routing follow-up; remote background workers are still deferred/disabled, so it is not part of this fixed remote cleanup RPC attack path.

