# Summary
Introduced an availability bug: unpaginated all-record cleanup listings are now sent over bounded RPC responses. Large attacker-controlled numbers of stream-upload sessions or completed multipart-upload tombstones can prevent cleanup and bucket deletion/pruning, allowing persistent resource exhaustion.
The new RPC methods `ObjectStreamUploadsList` and `ObjectCompletedMultipartUploadsList` return all matching records in a single response. Their encoders reject responses above `STORAGE_RPC_MAX_LIST_PAGE_ITEMS` and also build the full payload in memory before the frame-size check. The caller paths use these RPCs for stale stream-upload scavenging, bucket visible-data checks, and completed-multipart tombstone cleanup. Once an attacker with upload permission creates enough abandoned stream uploads or completed multipart-upload tombstones in a PG/bucket, the RPC response can exceed the item or frame limits. For best-effort stream cleanup the error is swallowed and the PG is skipped; for bucket deletion/pruning the error propagates, blocking cleanup. Before this commit these paths used local storage-client calls without the new RPC response cap, so this failure mode is introduced by routing the cleanup metadata over RPC without pagination.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the commit routed stream-upload and completed-multipart cleanup metadata through new RPC methods rather than only local storage-client paths.
- [x] Confirm the RPC server handlers collect all matching cleanup records and encode them as one response without pagination.
- [x] Confirm the response encoders enforce `STORAGE_RPC_MAX_LIST_PAGE_ITEMS` and reject oversized all-record lists.
- [x] Dynamically construct attacker-sized cleanup record sets and reproduce the exact `PayloadTooLarge` failure using the real target code.
- [x] Confirm caller impact: best-effort stream cleanup skips failed PGs, while bucket visible-data checks and completed-multipart cleanup/pruning propagate the error.
## Report
Validated the finding. The commit introduces `STORAGE_RPC_MAX_LIST_PAGE_ITEMS = 100000` at `crates/storage/src/storage_rpc.rs:125`. Both new cleanup-list response encoders reject all-record responses above that cap: stream uploads at `storage_rpc.rs:4126-4146` and completed multipart tombstones at `storage_rpc.rs:4166-4186`. The RPC server handlers load all matching local records into a Vec and pass them into these encoders in one response: `storage_node_server.rs:2838-2862` and `storage_node_server.rs:2865-2888`. Encoder errors are converted to `StorageNodeServerError::ResponsePayload` by dispatch (`storage_node_server.rs:1408-1413`), so the RPC session fails instead of returning a paginated page. The Unix client methods depend on these RPCs at `node_client.rs:7424-7437` and `node_client.rs:7440-7464`. Cleanup/deletion callers then either skip the PG (`cluster.rs:4647-4665`) or propagate the error into bucket drain/prune paths (`cluster/request_ops.rs:2333-2348`, `2380-2414`, `3060-3067`).

Dynamic PoC: I appended a temporary `#[cfg(test)]` module that constructs `100001` stream-upload records and `100001` completed-multipart records and calls the real encoders. Command: `CFLAGS='-O0 -g' CXXFLAGS='-O0 -g' cargo test -p storage autovalidator_cleanup_rpc_poc -- --nocapture`. Output showed both real encoders fail exactly at the cap: `stream upload list encoder error: storage RPC payload length 100001 exceeds limit 100000` and `completed multipart list encoder error: storage RPC payload length 100001 exceeds limit 100000`.

Crash/valgrind/debugger attempts: this is a controlled availability failure, not a memory-safety crash, so the PoC exits successfully after reproducing the blocking error. `valgrind` was not installed. ASan was attempted with `RUSTFLAGS='-Z sanitizer=address'`, but stable Rust rejected the nightly-only `-Z` flag. LLDB was available and used non-interactively; breakpoints at `storage::storage_rpc::encode_stream_uploads_list_response` and `storage::storage_rpc::encode_completed_multipart_uploads_list_response` produced backtraces showing the PoC invoking the exact encoder functions at `storage_rpc.rs:4129` and `storage_rpc.rs:4169` before returning the oversized-list errors.

